{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Walks childless.</b> Menachem ben Saruk explained it as a term of inheriting, and its companion is ״עֵר וְעֹנֶה״ (Malachi 2) — עֲרִירִי: without an heir, as you would say ״וּבְכָל תְּבוּאָתִי תְשָׁרֵשׁ״ (Job 31) — it uproots its roots; so too the term עֲרִירִי: lacking children; דישאנפנטיש בְּלַעַ\"ז. And to me it appears that עֵר וְעֹנֶה is from the root of ״וְלִבִּי עֵר״ (Song of Songs 5), and עֲרִירִי is a term of destruction — and so ״עָרוּ עָרוּ״ (Psalms 137), and so ״עָרוֹת יְסוֹד״ (Habakkuk 3), and so ״עַרְעֵר תִּתְעַרְעָר״ (Jeremiah 51), and so ״כִּי אַרְזָה עֵרָה״ (Zephaniah 2)." "<b>And the son of the steward of my house.</b> As the Targum renders — that my entire household is sustained by his mouth, as in ״וְעַל פִּיךָ יִשַּׁק״ (Genesis Kings 1): my steward; and if I had a son, my son would be appointed over what is mine." "<b>Of Damascus.</b> According to the Targum, he was from Damascus. And according to another interpretation: because the kings pursued as far as Damascus. And in the Talmud they expounded it as a notarikon: דּוֹלֶה וּמַשְׁקֶה — he drew and gave others to drink from his master's Torah."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French désherité, 'disinherited / heirless'", 1 "the citation 'בר' מלכים א' rendered literally; Rashi abbreviates — he means 1 Kings 12, the verse cited by its phrase"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:50.114178749Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}